'''''El Desig 2''''' ({{lang|ca|El Desig 2}}; literally "The Desire 2") is a 2010 Volisanian-language dark comedy film directed by [[Montserrat Riera i Sola]]. A subversive and idiosyncratic work, the film is not a sequel to Riera i Sola's earlier film ''[[El Desig (film)|El Desig]]'' (1987) in the conventional sense; rather than continuing the story of the original film, it depicts the obsessive investigation of a fictionalised prosecutor from [[Portblanc]] who is assigned to the ''El Desig'' obscenity case and becomes consumed by the need to establish a connection between the film and the conservative judicial district, all without ever watching the film himself. The film won the Best Film award at the [[Costa Nord Film Festival]] in 2010 and the top award at the [[Castejón Film Festival]] in 2011.

== Plot ==

The film follows Francesc, a conservative prosecutor in [[Portblanc]], [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]], who is assigned to the ''El Desig'' case after the [[Sant Cristòfor]] High Court dismisses the obscenity charges. Francesc is a morally rigid man who takes the prosecution with a seriousness disproportionate to the offence — a film seen by approximately two hundred people in a community arts centre — and he approaches the case as though it were a matter of national security.

Francesc refuses to watch the film. He believes that viewing it would compromise his prosecutorial objectivity, and he relies instead on written reports, witness statements, and secondhand accounts. His superiors and colleagues provide him with summaries of the film's content, and Francesc constructs his understanding of ''El Desig'' entirely from these fragmentary, often inaccurate descriptions. The gap between the film as it exists and the film as Francesc imagines it becomes the engine of the plot: his investigation is driven not by the film itself but by his projection of what the film might be.

Francesc's investigation centres on the question of jurisdiction. The case was re-filed at the Portblanc High Court because one of the actresses in ''El Desig'' was a Portblanc resident, and Francesc becomes obsessed with the idea that this jurisdictional connection is evidence of a broader link between the film and the conservative judicial district — a conspiracy, he suspects, between the underground film community and the Portblanc legal establishment. He pursues this theory through a series of increasingly absurd investigative steps: he interviews the actress's neighbours, he obtains her employment records, he maps the social networks of the harbour district's cultural organisations, and he compiles dossiers on individuals who have no apparent connection to the case.

During his investigation, Francesc meets Pau, a journalist from Sant Cristòfor who is covering the trial for a national newspaper. Pau is open, sociable, and intellectually curious — everything Francesc is not — and the two men enter into a romantic relationship. The film portrays the relationship with a deadpan irony that never acknowledges its central absurdity: a man prosecuting a film for its depictions of sexuality is engaged in a secret homosexual relationship that would, under the moral framework he is ostensibly defending, be at least as transgressive as anything depicted in the film. The relationship develops in parallel with Francesc's investigation, and the film intercuts the two storylines — the prosecutor's escalating obsession and his tender, clandestine romance — without drawing the connection explicitly.

Francesc's obsession leads him to break into the home of the filmmaker, Montserrat, searching for evidence of the conspiracy he has convinced himself exists. The break-in is depicted as both absurd and pathetic: a man driven by an obsession with a film he has never seen, rummaging through the personal belongings of a woman whose work he cannot bring himself to watch. The scene is staged with a quiet, uncomfortable comedy — Francesc finds nothing of evidentiary value, but he lingers in the apartment, examining the filmmaker's books, her editing equipment, and a poster from the original screening of ''El Desig'' at the Sant Cristòfor Cultural Centre.

The film's dark comic resolution arrives when Francesc, through a chance encounter at a family gathering, discovers that his own sister Mònica is one of the actresses in ''El Desig''. Mònica is a Portblanc resident — the very actress whose residency provided the jurisdictional basis for the re-filing of the charges that Francesc has been prosecuting. The connection Francesc has been searching for, the link between the film and Portblanc, has been in his own family all along. He has spent months conducting an obsessive investigation, breaking into a filmmaker's home, and pursuing a secret romance, all while the answer was a phone call to his sister away. Francesc never knew because he never watched the film and never asked his sister about her work.

The film ends without resolution. Francesc sits alone in his office, the dossiers and maps and witness statements spread across his desk, and the film holds on his face in a long, unbroken take. He does not watch the film. He does not call his sister. He does not resign from the case. The final shot is of the poster from the ''El Desig'' screening, which Francesc took from the filmmaker's apartment, now pinned to the wall of his office alongside his conspiracy maps.

== Cast ==

The cast was composed of professional and semi-professional actors, a departure from Riera i Sola's earlier practice of using non-professional performers. [[Bernat Puig i Colom]] played Francesc, the prosecutor; his performance, which required him to sustain the character's bureaucratic rigidity and repressed desire across a series of increasingly absurd situations, was widely praised by critics. [[Joana Serra i Bosch]] played Mònica, the prosecutor's sister and the actress in ''El Desig'', in a performance that was noted for its warmth and directness, which contrasted with Francesc's opacity. [[Oriol Mas i Fuster]] played Pau, the journalist and romantic interest. [[Aina Vives i Aragó]] played the filmmaker character, a fictionalised version of Riera i Sola herself, who appears in the film only in the sequences depicting the break-in and in background footage that Francesc watches as part of his investigation.

== Production ==

=== Background ===

Riera i Sola began developing ''El Desig 2'' in 2008, more than two decades after the original ''[[El Desig (film)|El Desig]]'' trial. The film was conceived as a dark comedy that would engage with the trial not through a courtroom dramatisation but through the story of one man's obsessive response to it. Riera i Sola has stated that she was interested in the gap between the film as it existed — a low-budget independent production seen by a small audience — and the film as it was imagined by the state apparatus that prosecuted it, and that the dark comedy format allowed her to explore that gap without the didacticism that a direct dramatisation of the trial would have required.

The break-in depicted in the film was inspired by a real incident. In the late 1980s, Riera i Sola's apartment was burgled, and several valuables were taken. The burglary was most likely an ordinary theft unconnected to the prosecution, but the incident was widely rumoured at the time to have been connected to the trial. Riera i Sola reimagined the break-in in ''El Desig 2'' as the act of the obsessive prosecutor, a fictionalisation that she has described as a way of taking ownership of a rumour that had circulated about her own life for two decades.

=== Financing ===

The film was produced with support from the Balboan Arts Council and an independent production company. The budget was modest by the standards of the mainstream Balboan film industry, which had recovered considerably since the [[1994 Balboa spot recession|1994 spot recession]], but it was significantly larger than the budgets of Riera i Sola's earlier independent films. The production employed a small crew and used digital video equipment, which had become accessible and affordable by the late 2000s and which allowed for a more flexible shooting process than the 16mm film of Riera i Sola's earlier work.

=== Filming ===

Principal photography took place over eight weeks in 2009. The film was shot on digital video using natural light and available interior lighting, in keeping with Riera i Sola's established aesthetic practice. The shooting locations included actual Portblanc courthouses, government offices, and residential apartments, as well as locations in Sant Cristòfor's harbour district. The break-in sequence was filmed in Riera i Sola's own apartment, using her actual belongings as props.

== Release ==

''El Desig 2'' was screened at the [[Costa Nord Film Festival]] in 2010 and received a limited theatrical release in Sant Cristòfor and other Balboan cities. The film was also screened at the UAC Awards ceremony. The theatrical release was the first time a film by Riera i Sola had been exhibited in commercial cinemas, a fact that was noted in the press coverage of the film's release.

== Reception ==

The film received critical acclaim. Critics praised the film's subversive structure, its tonal control, and the performance of Bernat Puig i Colom as Francesc. The film was described as a work of "sustained, uncomfortable comedy" by ''[[El Faro del Istmo]]'', and as "the most inventive Balboan film of the year" by ''[[La Veu de la Badia]]''. The film's treatment of the relationship between Francesc and Pau was noted for its restraint and its refusal to editorialise, which allowed the audience to draw the ironic connections without being instructed to do so.

The film attracted controversy in the interior provinces, where some commentators considered it a trivialisation of the moral concerns that had motivated the original prosecution. Critics in Sant Cristòfor and the cultural press generally regarded the film as a deft act of retrospective reckoning — a work that used comedy to examine the institutional absurdity of the trial without diminishing the seriousness of the constitutional questions it had raised.

At the [[Costa Nord Film Festival]] in 2010, ''El Desig 2'' won the Best Film award, the festival's highest honour. The following year, the film won the top award at the [[Castejón Film Festival]] in [[Castejón]], [[Balisca]], making it the first Balboan film to win the top award at both festivals. The Castejón award was regarded as particularly significant, as the Baliscan film community had historically been closely connected to the Balboan film industry through the Baliscano Balboan diaspora, and the award was interpreted as a recognition of Riera i Sola's contribution to the broader Baliscan-language cinematic tradition.

Despite the critical acclaim and the festival awards, ''El Desig 2'' achieved only limited commercial success. The film's strangeness — its subversive structure, its refusal to conform to the conventions of either the courtroom drama or the comedy sequel, and its sustained tone of uncomfortable absurdist humour — limited its appeal to a mass audience. The film grossed approximately $247,000 against a production budget of approximately $72,000, a modest return that reflected the difficulty of marketing a film whose premises resisted easy summarisation. The theatrical run was brief: the film played on a limited number of screens in Sant Cristòfor for approximately five weeks, and the festival awards did not produce a significant expansion of the audience. The film's commercial performance was regarded as consistent with the broader pattern of Riera i Sola's career — critical recognition without commercial breakthrough — though the festival awards distinguished ''El Desig 2'' from her earlier works, which had not received theatrical distribution at all.

In the years following its release, ''El Desig 2'' developed a cult following. The film's strangeness, which had limited its commercial appeal, became the basis of its enduring reputation: the film was rediscovered by younger viewers through home video and streaming platforms, and its subversive structure and absurdist humour attracted a dedicated audience that appreciated the film's refusal to resolve its own premises. The film's cult status was reinforced by the growing scholarly attention to Riera i Sola's work in the 2010s, which examined the film's treatment of institutional obsession, sexual hypocrisy, and the gap between state power and personal knowledge.

== Legacy ==

''El Desig 2'' is regarded as Riera i Sola's most accessible film and, despite its limited commercial success, her most widely seen work. Its critical acclaim and festival awards brought her work to a wider audience than her earlier films had reached, and the film is credited with introducing a new generation of Balboan viewers to the history of the ''El Desig'' trial and the Gilded age underground. The film's subsequent cult following — driven by its strangeness, its subversive structure, and its refusal to conform to commercial expectations — sustained its reputation beyond its brief theatrical run and established it as a distinctive work in the history of Balboan cinema. The film's subversive structure — a "sequel" that is not a sequel, a courtroom drama without a courtroom, a comedy about a trial that never depicts the trial — has been the subject of analysis by film scholars, who have examined the film's treatment of institutional obsession, sexual hypocrisy, and the gap between state power and personal knowledge.

The film's success at the Costa Nord and Castejón film festivals was regarded as a vindication of Riera i Sola's career-long commitment to independent filmmaking. The UAC, which had belatedly recognised Riera i Sola with a retrospective in 2002 and a Lifetime Achievement Award in 2003, noted that the festival awards confirmed the artistic significance that the organisation had been among the first to acknowledge.

